            <ArticleTitle>Cholesterol excretion and colon cancer.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>3738-40</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Populations consuming diets high in fat and cholesterol exhibit a greater incidence of colon cancer than those consuming less fat and cholesterol. Lowering elevated serum cholesterol levels experimentally or clinically is associated with increased large-bowel tumorigenesis. Thus, cholesterol lost to the gut, either dietary or endogenously synthesized, appears to have a role in large-bowel cancer. Whether the effect(s) is mediated by increases in fecal bile acid excretion or some other mechanism is not clear.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
